The Three Configuration Sources

• Local File: The YAML or JSON file on your machine that defines your desired state.
• Last-Applied Configuration: A JSON string stored inside the live object's metadata under the annotation kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration. It records what the local file looked like the last time apply was run.
• Live Object Configuration: The current, active configuration of the resource running inside the Kubernetes cluster (stored in etcd), which includes default values and fields added by controllers or manual edits.


1. How kubectl apply Creates an Object

When you run kubectl apply -f manifest.yaml for a resource that does not exist yet in the cluster, Kubernetes follows these steps:
• Checks the cluster: kubectl queries the Kubernetes API server and sees that no matching object exists.
• Injects the annotation: Before sending the manifest to the API server, kubectl serializes the entire contents of your local file into JSON and saves it inside the kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation.
• Creates the live object: The API server creates the resource in the cluster, populates any default fields (like imagePullPolicy or restartPolicy), and stores both the live configuration and the new annotation.


2. How kubectl apply Modifies an Object (Three-Way Merge)

When you update your local file and run kubectl apply -f manifest.yaml again, kubectl performs a three-way diff between the local file, the last-applied configuration, and the live object to build a patch.


2. How kubectl apply Modifies an Object (Three-Way Merge)

When you update your local file and run kubectl apply -f manifest.yaml again, kubectl performs a three-way diff between the local file, the last-applied configuration, and the live object to build a patch.

Scenario	                 Comparison Logic	                Result on the Live Object
Adding a field -> Present in local file, missing in last-applied and live object. ->	The new field is added to the live object.

Updating a field -> Value in local file differs from last-applied and live object. -> The field is updated to match your new local file value.

Deleting a field -> Present in last-applied, removed from local file. -> The field is explicitly deleted from the live object.

**Important

Preserving unmanaged fields -> Present in live object (e.g., added by an autoscaler or manual kubectl edit), missing in both local file and last-applied. -> The field is left untouched because kubectl knows it did not manage it previously.

***
• Updates the annotation: After calculating and applying the strategic merge patch to the live object, kubectl overwrites the last-applied-configuration annotation with the latest contents of your local file so it is ready for the next run.

