why do we need last applied configuration file then?

last applied configuration is not updated when object is updated via create command(and object created using apply)

why should kubernetes compare both live object and last applied configuration

Kubernetes must compare both the live object and the last-applied configuration because comparing only one makes it impossible to distinguish between a field you intentionally deleted and a field that Kubernetes added automatically.

If kubectl apply only compared your local file against the live object (a two-way diff), two major problems would occur:


1. The Problem of Deleting Fields

Imagine your live object has a field like tier: frontend, which is missing from your local file. Without the last-applied-configuration, kubectl cannot tell which of these two scenarios happened:

• Scenario A (You deleted it): You previously had tier: frontend in your local file, applied it, and just deleted that line from your YAML. kubectl should delete it from the cluster.

• Scenario B (Kubernetes added it): Kubernetes or another controller (like an autoscaler or service mesh) automatically injected tier: frontend into the live object. You never had it in your file, so kubectl should not delete it.

How the 3rd file solves it: kubectl checks the last-applied-configuration. 
If tier: frontend was in the last-applied file, it knows you owned it and just deleted it (Scenario A).
If tier: frontend wasn't in the last-applied file, it knows it was not created originally by it and it is created by a controller added it and leaves it alone (Scenario B).


2. Why Not Compare Only Local vs. Last-Applied?

You might wonder why kubectl doesn't just ignore the live object and only compare your local file against the last-applied-configuration.

• Drift Detection: Someone might have manually edited the live cluster using kubectl edit or kubectl scale (causing "configuration drift").
• Enforcing Desired State: If someone manually changed replicas: 3 to replicas: 1 directly on the live cluster, your local file (replicas: 3) and last-applied (replicas: 3) would look identical. Without checking the live object, kubectl would think nothing changed and fail to fix the drift back to 3 replicas.